---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { FREE_TO_RUN_LINE } from '../../data/product-facts.mjs';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is IISpeed still supported?',
    a: 'Support continues for existing customers, but IISpeed is deprecated and bug fixes and security patches have stopped. New IIS deployments should use mod_pagespeed 1.15 for IIS, and ASP.NET Core deployments should use the WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar NuGet package.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I hold an IISpeed license. What do I need to do?',
    a: 'Nothing to move to mod_pagespeed 1.15 or the WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar NuGet package — both are free to install and run. Questions about an existing IISpeed subscription go to license@we-amp.com.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What replaces IISpeed for IIS deployments?',
    a: 'mod_pagespeed 1.15 ships a native IIS module with the same web.config surface IISpeed offered — a modernized, secured filter stack with new optimizers, same admin console, plus security patches, current toolchain, and Cyclone Cache.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar work with classic ASP.NET?',
    a: "WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar is for ASP.NET Core. For classic ASP.NET / WebForms / MVC 5 on IIS, mod_pagespeed 1.15 for IIS is the right path — it's an IIS module, no application code changes required.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I run both mod_pagespeed 1.15 for IIS and the WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar package?',
    a: 'Yes. Mixed environments are supported. Both run the mod_pagespeed 1.15 core — the native IIS module and the Sidecar package (bundled nginx + ngx_pagespeed on loopback) — so they share the same filters and admin console.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
    name: 'mod_pagespeed 1.15 — IIS port',
    applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
    applicationSubCategory: 'IIS module',
    operatingSystem: ['Windows'],
    description:
      'Native IIS module that continues the IISpeed lineage. Same web.config surface, plus security patches, current toolchain, and Cyclone Cache.',
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'We-Amp B.V.',
      url: 'https://www.we-amp.com/',
    },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
    name: 'WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar (ASP.NET Core integration)',
    applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
    operatingSystem: ['Linux'],
    softwareRequirements: 'ASP.NET Core 6 or newer',
    description:
      'GA 1.15 ASP.NET Core integration: your Kestrel app is the public front door, and the bundled nginx (1.30.2) runs on loopback behind it as the optimize-proxy. Image optimization, CSS/JS minification, and critical CSS, powered by the mod_pagespeed 1.15 core.',
    sameAs: 'https://www.nuget.org/packages/WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar',
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'We-Amp B.V.',
      url: 'https://www.we-amp.com/',
    },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((entry) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: entry.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: entry.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="IISpeed successor: PageSpeed for IIS (2026)"
  description="IISpeed is deprecated. Its successor is mod_pagespeed 1.15, the native IIS module — free license transfer, or the Sidecar NuGet package for ASP.NET Core."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-20">
    <header>
      <h1 class="h1-hero text-text-body">IISpeed alternative</h1>
      <p class="lede mt-6">
        IISpeed is deprecated because We-Amp consolidated two IIS code paths into one (mod_pagespeed
        1.15). Existing customers get a free license transfer. IISpeed was We-Amp's native IIS port
        of mod_pagespeed, first released in 2012 &mdash; image optimization, CSS/JS minification,
        and critical CSS injection on the IIS pipeline for Windows-hosted sites.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          href="/1.1/docs/downloads/"
          class="btn-primary"
          data-umami-event="cta_alternatives_iispeed_install"
          >Download &amp; run mod_pagespeed 1.15</a
        >
        <a
          href="/pricing/"
          class="rounded-md border border-border-strong px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-text-body hover:border-border-strong"
          data-umami-event="cta_alternatives_iispeed_pricing">Support plans</a
        >
        <a
          href="mailto:license@we-amp.com?subject=IISpeed license transfer"
          class="rounded-md border border-border-strong px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-text-body hover:border-border-strong"
          >Transfer my IISpeed license</a
        >
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-annotation">
        {FREE_TO_RUN_LINE}
      </p>
    </header>

    <div
      class="mt-12 prose prose-stone max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-a:text-interactive dark:prose-invert"
    >
      <p>
        IISpeed is now deprecated. Existing customers get a free license transfer to either
        mod_pagespeed 1.15 (native IIS port) or to the WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar NuGet package for
        ASP.NET Core. Support continues, but bug fixes and security patches have stopped.
      </p>
      <p>This page explains both forward paths.</p>

      <h2>What IISpeed was</h2>
      <p>
        IISpeed wrapped the mod_pagespeed optimization libraries in an IIS module (<code
          >IISpeedModule.dll</code
        >) that registered against the IIS request pipeline. Configuration sat in <code
          >web.config</code
        > under <code>&lt;system.webServer&gt;</code>. Filters, variants, and the admin console all
        worked the same way as the Apache and nginx ports.
      </p>
      <p>
        Customers ran it on Windows Server 2008 R2 through 2019. The IIS module ABI stayed
        compatible across those releases.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it's deprecated</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The .NET 4 and IIS 7-era toolchain is increasingly painful to build and test against
          modern Windows.
        </li>
        <li>
          The current image pipeline (WebP variants), Core Web Vitals signals, and the newer variant
          fan-out never landed in the IISpeed build.
        </li>
        <li>
          Customer demand consolidated on either drop-in IIS replacements (where 1.15 fits) or on
          ASP.NET Core (where the WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar NuGet package fits).
        </li>
        <li>
          One IIS code path is better than two. The IIS port now lives inside
          <code>mod_pagespeed 1.15</code>, shared with the Apache and nginx ports (Envoy support is
          experimental).
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Path 1 &mdash; mod_pagespeed 1.15 for IIS</h2>
      <p>
        If you have an IIS deployment and want a drop-in replacement, <code>mod_pagespeed 1.15</code
        >
        has a native IIS port with the same module surface IISpeed offered. Same <code
          >web.config</code
        >
        syntax, a modernized, secured filter stack with new optimizers, same admin console &mdash; plus
        security patches, current toolchain, and Cyclone Cache.
      </p>

      <pre><code>{`<!-- web.config -->
<system.webServer>
  <modules>
    <add name="ModPagespeedModule" type="ModPagespeed.IIS.Module, ModPagespeed.IIS" />
  </modules>
  <pagespeed enabled="true">
    <fileCachePath>C:\\inetpub\\modpagespeed\\cache</fileCachePath>
  </pagespeed>
</system.webServer>`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        The MSI installer from the
        <a href="/1.1/docs/downloads/">downloads page</a> registers the module, creates the cache directory,
        and warm-starts the worker.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/1.1/docs/">mod_pagespeed 1.15 docs</a> &middot;
        <a href="/mod-pagespeed-still-maintained/">IIS migration guide</a> &middot;
        <a href="/1.1/docs/downloads/">Download the MSI</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Path 2 &mdash; WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar NuGet package for ASP.NET Core</h2>
      <p>
        If your stack is ASP.NET Core, IIS is no longer the natural integration point. The
        <code>WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar</code> NuGet package is the GA 1.15 ASP.NET Core integration: your
        Kestrel app stays the public front door, and the package's bundled nginx runs on loopback behind
        it as the optimize-proxy:
      </p>

      <pre><code>{`// Program.cs
using WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddPageSpeed();

var app = builder.Build();
app.UsePageSpeed();
app.Run();`}</code></pre>

      <pre><code>dotnet add package WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar</code></pre>

      <p>
        The package bundles the mod_pagespeed 1.15 core (nginx 1.30.2) and starts it on loopback
        automatically &mdash; no IIS module registration, no <code>web.config</code> editing, no
        <code>gacutil</code>. It is Linux-only (linux-x64 / linux-arm64), for container and Linux
        deployments of ASP.NET Core.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/blog/aspnet-core-middleware/">How the integration works</a> &middot;
        <a href="/go/nuget?pkg=sidecar&from=alternatives-iispeed-inline">NuGet package</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Which path fits</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Use case</th><th>Choose</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Classic ASP.NET / WebForms / MVC 5 on IIS</td>
            <td>mod_pagespeed 1.15 for IIS</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Existing IISpeed install you want to keep running with security patches</td>
            <td>mod_pagespeed 1.15 for IIS</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>ASP.NET Core 6+ on Kestrel (Linux)</td>
            <td>WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar NuGet package</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Linux container deployment of an ASP.NET Core app</td>
            <td>WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar NuGet package</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>ASP.NET Core hosted on Windows / IIS</td>
            <td>mod_pagespeed 1.15 for IIS (the Sidecar is Linux-only)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mixed environment (some sites on classic IIS, some on .NET Core)</td>
            <td>Run both &mdash; they share the same core and admin console</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>License transfer</h2>
      <p>
        If you hold an IISpeed license, there is nothing to transfer: mod_pagespeed 1.15 and the
        WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar NuGet package are free to install and run. Questions about an
        existing IISpeed subscription go to <a href="mailto:license@we-amp.com"
          >license@we-amp.com</a
        >; the IISpeed transition window is documented at
        <a href="https://iispeed.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">iispeed.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      {
        faq.map((entry) => (
          <>
            <h3>{entry.q}</h3>
            <p>{entry.a}</p>
          </>
        ))
      }

      <h2>Install and run</h2>
      <p>
        Install either path and it optimizes right away. The converged mod_pagespeed 2.1 is open
        source under Apache-2.0 &mdash; free in development and in production.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>mod_pagespeed 1.15 for IIS</strong> &mdash;
          <a href="/1.1/docs/downloads/">Download the MSI</a> &middot; <a href="/pricing/"
            >Support plans</a
          > &middot;
          <a href="/1.1/docs/iis-configuration/">IIS docs</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar NuGet package</strong> &mdash;
          <a href="/go/nuget?pkg=sidecar&from=alternatives-iispeed-tryone">NuGet package</a> &middot;
          <a href="/docs/aspnet-getting-started/">ASP.NET Core docs</a>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/alternatives/mod-pagespeed/">mod_pagespeed alternative</a></li>
        <li><a href="/alternatives/ngx-pagespeed/">ngx_pagespeed alternative</a></li>
        <li>
          <a href="/alternatives/google-pagespeed-module/">Google PageSpeed Module alternative</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/blog/mod-pagespeed-deprecated-2026/"
            >Is mod_pagespeed deprecated? &mdash; what changed and what to use</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://iispeed.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">iispeed.com</a> &mdash; transition
          information for existing customers
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://iispeed.com/iis-core-web-vitals-2026/" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >IIS &amp; Core Web Vitals in 2026</a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-xs text-text-annotation">
        IIS, ASP.NET, Windows Server, and other product names are trademarks of their respective
        owners. mod_pagespeed is an open-source project originally developed at Google; its
        maintained continuation, mod_pagespeed 2.1, is developed by We-Amp B.V. Comparisons reflect
        publicly available information as of 2026 and are provided for evaluation; We-Amp B.V. is
        not affiliated with or endorsed by Microsoft Corporation or Google.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
